# Interpretation

## The Literal Story

This is a song about a tiny restaurant deep in the forest that serves **the dead** — or more precisely, it's a restaurant at the boundary of death, and the narrator is someone who has just arrived there (i.e., just died).

**Verse 1:** The narrator says they can't get there by following acorns (the trail of acorns — the path animals would follow — doesn't lead there). Instead, they arrive by "fiddling with empty pockets" and "arriving from someone who forgot" (忘れた人から辿り着く). This is the key: you reach this restaurant not by walking, but by *being forgotten* — by the living. The narrator has died, and as the living gradually forget them, they slip further along until they arrive. Their pockets are empty because you take nothing with you.

**Verse 2:** The restaurant has no reservations — because nobody *plans* to come here. The lunch hour is full of empty seats, and the little birds are laughing/fluttering (パタパタ) — the forest creatures find it amusing, because they know what this place is.

**Verse 3:** The corrugated tin roof painted bright red — a cheerful, almost childlike image. The menu is "only recommendations" (おすすめ そればかり) — you don't choose here; death serves you what it serves. From the kitchen come violin, flute, cello, viola — the "cooking sounds" are actually music, the kind of music played at a funeral or a send-off.

**Chorus:** "Welcome, welcome! Eat your fill, then sleep. And now, everyone, goodbye. Tomorrow is tomorrow, et cetera." This is the restaurant's whole speech: eat your last meal, then rest (die/sleep), goodbye, and "tomorrow is tomorrow, etc." — a breezy dismissal of whatever comes next. The lightheartedness is the point: death here is treated as casual hospitality.

**Final verse:** Guests pour in from right to left in droves (おおわらわ) — the recently dead arriving en masse. Even the pet bear cub on someone's hand starts dancing. The dishes are listed — carpaccio, paella, hors d'oeuvres, risotto — but **"there is no dessert."** Because there is no "after" after this. Then the devastating final lines:

> お墓の中まで届けましょう / 今宵は最後のフルコース
> "We'll deliver it right into your grave / Tonight is the last full course"

The restaurant delivers its final course *into the grave*. The narrator's last meal is served where they will be buried. Tonight's full course is the last one they will ever eat.

## Why It Works as a Literal Story

In a fantastical setting, this is simply a restaurant that exists in the afterlife (or the moment just before it), staffed by forest spirits, where the dead are fed one final feast before sleeping. The animals can talk and laugh, a bear cub dances on a hand, and the kitchen plays chamber music instead of cooking sounds. Nothing needs to be read as metaphor — it's a whimsical afterlife diner.

## The Tone

The genius of the lyrics is the mismatch between subject and mood. The melody is mischievous and light; the content is a last supper before oblivion. The restaurant staff aren't mournful — they're cheerful hosts. "Tomorrow is tomorrow, et cetera" (明日は明日で エトセトラ) is the most chilling line precisely because it's so breezy: there is no tomorrow worth specifying, so it's just "etc."

It reads like a Japanese cousin of the "Devil's tavern at the crossroads" trope, but gentler — more like a **Grave of the Fireflies meets Ghibli's whimsy**, or a forest version of a *yomotsu-hegui* (the food of the underworld in Japanese myth, which binds you to the land of the dead once eaten). Eat your fill here, and you sleep — and don't wake up in the world you came from. [end of text]


